        "interference-pattern"
      ],
      "presentation_role": "SOURCE_BACKED_EXPLANATION",
      "semantic_tier": "SOURCE_BACKED_NON_CANONICAL",
      "short_label": "Source explanation 3",
      "statement": "Variations of the double-slit experiment using electrons and atoms produce the same type of interference pattern."
    },
    {
      "assertion_id": "assertion-49c2a9a32b078712",
      "directional": false,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "document_id": "scope-eb184226638e5cbb",
          "end_char": 1175,
          "quote": "If the source intensity is turned down, the same interference pattern will slowly build up, one \"count\" or particle (e.g. photon or electron) at a time.",
          "start_char": 1023
        }
      ],
      "id": "explanation-49c2a9a32b078712",
      "participant_entity_ids": [
        "interference-pattern",
        "photon",
        "electron"
      ],
      "presentation_role": "SOURCE_BACKED_EXPLANATION",
      "semantic_tier": "SOURCE_BACKED_NON_CANONICAL",
      "short_label": "Source explanation 4",
      "statement": "When the source intensity is reduced, the interference pattern builds up one particle at a time, including photons or electrons."
    },
    {
      "assertion_id": "assertion-a4ca636403e86833",
      "directional": false,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "document_id": "scope-eb184226638e5cbb",
          "end_char": 1287,
          "quote": "The quantum system acts as a wave when passing through the double slits, but as a particle when it is detected.",
          "start_char": 1176
        }
      ],
      "id": "explanation-a4ca636403e86833",
      "participant_entity_ids": [
        "double-slit-experiment",
        "principle-of-complementarity"
      ],
      "presentation_role": "SOURCE_BACKED_EXPLANATION",
      "semantic_tier": "SOURCE_BACKED_NON_CANONICAL",
      "short_label": "Source explanation 5",
      "statement": "In the double-slit experiment, the quantum system acts as a wave when passing through the double slits and as a particle when detected."
    },
    {
